### **Complexity Analysis**

Let m be the number of rows and n be the number of columns.

For every element in arr:

- Finding its position takes O(m \* n).
- Checking its row takes O(n).
- Checking its column takes O(m).

There are m \* n elements in arr.

**Time Complexity:**

O(m \* n \* (m \* n + m + n))

\= O((m \* n)²)

**Space Complexity:**

O(m \* n)

The visited matrix requires O(m \* n) space.

**Problem:** We repeatedly search the entire matrix to find the position of each number. This is unnecessary work.


class Solution {
public:
    int firstCompleteIndex(vector<int>& arr, vector<vector<int>>& mat) {
        int m = mat.size();
        int n = mat[0].size();

        vector<vector<int>> vis(m, vector<int>(n, 0));
         for (int k = 0; k < arr.size(); k++)
         {
            int x = arr[k];
            int r = -1, c = -1;

            for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {

                for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                    
                    if (mat[i][j] == x) {
                        r = i;
                        c = j;
                        break;
                    }
                }
                if (r != -1) break;
            }

            vis[r][c] = 1;

            // Check complete row
            bool rowComplete = true;

            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                if (vis[r][j] == 0) {
                    rowComplete = false;
                    break;
                }
            }

            // Check complete column
            bool colComplete = true;

            for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
                if (vis[i][c] == 0) {
                    colComplete = false;
                    break;
                }
            }

            if (rowComplete || colComplete)
                return k;
         }
         return -1;

    }
};

//Better — Hash Map + Simulation 
### **Complexity Analysis**

| Operation                | Complexity        |
| ------------------------ | ----------------- |
| Build position map       | O(m \* n) average |
| Traverse arr             | O(m \* n) average |
| Update row/column counts | O(1) per element  |

**Time Complexity:**

O(m \* n)

**Space Complexity:**

O(m \* n)

The position map takes O(m \* n) space, and row/column counters take O(m + n).

This is already an optimal asymptotic time complexity.

class Solution {
public:
    int firstCompleteIndex(vector<int>& arr, vector<vector<int>>& mat) {

        int m = mat.size();
        int n = mat[0].size();

        unordered_map<int, pair<int, int>> pos;

        // Store positions of all matrix elements
        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                pos[mat[i][j]] = {i, j};
            }
        }

        vector<int> rowCount(m, 0);
        vector<int> colCount(n, 0);

        for (int k = 0; k < arr.size(); k++) {

            int x = arr[k];

            int r = pos[x].first;
            int c = pos[x].second;

            rowCount[r]++;
            colCount[c]++;

            if (rowCount[r] == n || colCount[c] == m) {
                return k;
            }
        }

        return -1;
    }
};


Optimal — Array Mapping + Counting
| Complexity      | Value    |
| --------------- | -------- |
| Time            | O(m × n) |
| Auxiliary Space | O(m × n) |

This is optimal because we need to process the matrix and the painting sequence, both containing m × n elements.

class Solution {
public:
    int firstCompleteIndex(vector<int>& arr, vector<vector<int>>& mat) {

        int m = mat.size();
        int n = mat[0].size();

        vector<pair<int, int>> pos(m * n + 1);

        // Store row and column of every number
        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                pos[mat[i][j]] = {i, j};
            }
        }

        vector<int> rowCount(m, 0);
        vector<int> colCount(n, 0);

        // Paint elements according to arr
        for (int k = 0; k < arr.size(); k++) {

            int r = pos[arr[k]].first;
            int c = pos[arr[k]].second;

            rowCount[r]++;
            colCount[c]++;

            if (rowCount[r] == n || colCount[c] == m) {
                return k;
            }
        }

        return -1;
    }
};
